course: dictionary
lesson: 10968
title: Wander: walking, drifting and daydreaming
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To wander means to walk around slowly without a clear plan or a place to be. Read the context: «The hedgehog wandering about in winter snow has woken too early.» The hedgehog is not heading anywhere in particular. It is moving about slowly, and the snow tells you it is probably lost or in trouble.
explain: Wander can also mean to go away from where you should be, often without meaning to. «Don't wander off» is a key rule on trips, at the beach, in malls and in forests. The clue is in the rest of the advice: stay with your group, and if you get lost, stay where you are and ask a safe adult.
explain: Your mind and eyes can wander too. «My mind wandered in the lesson» does not mean your brain went for a walk. It means your thoughts drifted away and you stopped paying attention. Quick check: swap in drifted or stopped paying attention. If the sentence still makes sense, you have found the right meaning.
fact: «Wander» means to walk around slowly without a clear plan.
fact: «Wander off» means to go away from where you should be, often without meaning to.
fact: A «wander» (a noun, informal in the UK) is a slow, relaxed walk.
fact: If your mind wanders, you stop paying attention and think about other things.
fact: In stories, characters often wander into forests and find adventures.
exercise: «The hedgehog wandering about in winter snow has woken too early.» What does wandering mean here? | *Walking about with no clear plan | Sleeping deeply in a nest of leaves | Singing loudly | Flying high over the trees
exercise: In a safety lesson, «Don't wander off» means what? | *Don't go away from your group | Don't walk slowly | Don't ask a safe adult for help | Don't sing on the trip
exercise: «My mind wandered in the lesson.» What does this mean? | I walked slowly around the room | My mind was very clever and solved a puzzle | I left the classroom to go outside | *My thoughts drifted away and I stopped paying attention
test: «We went for a wander along the river, with no hurry at all.» In this sentence, what is a wander? | A long nap after lunch | A fast race to the finish | *A slow, relaxed walk | A loud shout across the field
test: «Stay where you are and ask a safe adult.» Why is this the rule if you get lost? | Hiding behind a tree keeps you safe | Walking on until you find the way is always best | Running fast helps you find your group | *Staying put helps a safe adult find you
exam: In «the tense that wandered from "went" to "go"», wandered means: | Walked slowly around a park | *Drifted away from the correct form, often without meaning to | Stayed exactly the same | Hid under a bed
